From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:39:06 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Implement wlr-layer-shell-unstable-v1

Add a zwlr_layer_shell_v1 global (version 5) and a layer surface role.

A layer surface is a shell surface backed by a MetaWindowWayland, like
an xdg_toplevel, so it goes through the normal window management code:

- Background layer surfaces are desktop windows, the other layers are
  docks, so they are on all workspaces, skip the task bar and the pager,
  are not placed and are left alone by the onscreen constraints.
- The stack layer follows the layer: background -> DESKTOP, bottom ->
  BOTTOM, top -> DOCK (BOTTOM while the monitor has a fullscreen
  window, as for docks), overlay -> OVERRIDE_REDIRECT.
- The size and position come from the anchors, size, margins and the
  exclusive zones of the other layer surfaces on the output. A size of
  0 fills the space between the anchored edges. They are applied with
  meta_window_move_resize(), which sends the configure through the
  shell surface configure vfunc; the acked commit completes it through
  meta_window_wayland_finish_move_resize().
- A positive exclusive zone becomes a strut on the window, so the work
  area shrinks around it. Layer surfaces with an exclusive zone of 0
  are placed inside the area left by the others; -1 ignores them.
- Keyboard interactivity none makes the window not focusable;
  on_demand and exclusive surfaces take focus on click (new
  MetaWindow::dock_click_focus), and exclusive surfaces on the top and
  overlay layers are focused when they map.
- The closed event is sent when the output goes away or the window is
  closed.

meta_window_set_struts() lets the layer shell set struts directly,
since they don't come from window properties.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
